{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RgPhZ4nadRZ9DY3ha1ZnsN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose tasks for release 00.0.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development. It derives formal specs from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against reference GNU binaries.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - Specifications\n\n**Current State:**\n- Generated code exists at tagged branches but NOT on the working branch\n- All releases from 00.0 through 01.3 and 99.x have specs written but code status is \"not started\" on the current branch\n- The code_status shows all use cases as \"codestatus: not started\"\n\n**Release 00.0 - Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure:**\nUse cases:\n1. rel00.0-uc001-magefiles - spec_complete, code not started\n2. rel00.0-uc002-format - spec_complete, code not started\n3. rel00.0-uc003-sys - spec_complete, code not started\n4. rel00.0-uc004-testutils - spec_complete, code not started\n\n**Constraints:**\n- Only propose tasks for release 00.0\n- Maximum 1 task\n- Do NOT propose tasks for magefiles/ (excluded by DD7)\n- Target 150-250 lines, 5-7 files, max 4 requirements\n- Must anchor to PRD requirement IDs\n\nFrom release 00.0, I'm looking at the available use cases: magefiles is excluded by DD7, leaving format, sys, and testutils as candidates. Following the dependency ordering principle, I need to prioritize foundational libraries before consumers, so I'm evaluating which of these three should be the first task to implement.\n\nLooking at the dependency relationships, testutils, sys, and format are actually independent of each other—none of them depend on the others. Since I can only propose one task, I need to determine which is most fundamental to the overall system.\n\nTestutils is the differential testing harness that serves as the core verification mechanism for all cmd/ packages, making it the most critical foundation to establish first.\n\nNow I need to locate the PRD for testutils to reference in my task. The context indicates PRDs are stored in docs/specs/product-requirements/ with a naming pattern like prd[NNN]-<utility>.yaml, and testutils should correspond to the use case rel00.0-uc004-testutils. Since I don't have the full PRD content available, I'll need to work with what I can infer from the architecture.\n\nLooking at the architecture more carefully, testutils is responsible for executing a Go binary and a reference GNU binary with identical inputs, then comparing their stdout, stderr, and exit codes byte-for-byte or with configured normalization. The file structure follows a specific convention where the primary type name determines the filename—so if DiffTest is the primary type, the file should be difftest.go rather than testutils.go.\n\nGiven that I can only propose one task, I need to decide between implementing the core testutils package itself or setting up its infrastructure. Since testutils is the differential testing harness that everything else depends on, I should focus on establishing that foundation first.\n\nPer the guidelines, I need to split implementation from tests into separate tasks, and implementation should come before tests. So I'm proposing the pkg/testutils/ implementation task specifically, anchored to the relevant PRD requirements by ID.\n\nThe DiffTest struct needs to capture a single differential test case with fields for the input arguments, stdin, environment variables, expected exit code, and expected output. The core functionality is executing two binaries with identical inputs and comparing their stdout, stderr, and exit codes, then reporting any divergences along with the input and actual output. I'll need to reference the testutils PRD from the docs/specs/product-requirements/ directory and potentially the test suite specs as well.\n\nFor the implementation, I'm planning a focused approach: define the DiffTest struct with all necessary input fields, implement the binary execution logic that runs both binaries in parallel with the same environment, add comparison logic for the three output dimensions, and support normalization hooks to handle known acceptable differences. This should fit within the 150-250 line budget by keeping it to the core four requirements without extra features.\n\nI'm second-guessing the PRD filename though. The status shows rel01.0 for the spec but the actual code is tagged at rel00.0-uc004-testutils, so there's a version mismatch I need to clarify. Looking at the naming convention, it should follow prd[NNN]-testutils.yaml, but I need to verify which PRD number corresponds to this utility before I can properly reference it. Looking at the project structure, I can see the docs are organized with PRDs in product-requirements, use cases in use-cases, and test suites in test-suites, but I don't have the exact numbering for the testutils PRD yet. I'll reference the architecture document and use case directly since those are more predictable, and let the stitch agent glob for the PRD if needed. I need to anchor the task to specific PRD requirement IDs, but I don't have access to the actual PRD files—only the architecture description and use case documentation. Since I can't read the PRD content directly, I'll ground the task in the architecture and use case references, and include the PRD in the required reading so the stitch agent can pull the specific requirement IDs from there.\n\nThe most foundational piece to tackle is the pkg/testutils/ implementation, so I'll propose a single task focused on that with the use case ID as the anchor point.\n\nSince the DiffTest struct and runner implementation should fit within the line budget, I can combine the types and implementation into one task rather than splitting them. Let me draft this as a cohesive unit that includes both the struct definition and the differential test runner logic.\n\nLooking at the file structure, the primary file should be difftest.go per the naming convention, though I might need to split the comparison and normalization logic into separate files depending on how the implementation grows. Let me reconsider whether everything can stay consolidated or if I need multiple files.\n\nFor the core requirements, I need to define the DiffTest struct with fields for input arguments, stdin, environment variables, and expected outputs, then implement a runner that executes both the Go binary and reference binary with identical inputs. The comparison logic needs to check stdout, stderr, and exit codes while reporting any divergences, and I should support normalization hooks to handle known acceptable differences. Looking at the architecture requirements, the DiffTest struct needs fields for input, arguments, stdin, environment variables, expected exit code, and expected output—all defined in difftest.go per the P7 naming convention. I'm also noting that the PRD number for testutils isn't immediately clear, so I'll reference the architecture document directly and let the stitch agent locate the corresponding PRD in the specs directory. I'm looking at the task structure for implementing the DiffTest struct and runner in pkg/testutils, which requires reading the architecture docs, finding the relevant PRD file, reviewing the use case specification, and checking the test suite to understand what needs to be covered. The main deliverable is creating the difftest.go file with the implementation. I need to add support for normalization hooks that let callers apply custom transformations to output before comparison, which handles cases like timestamps that differ legitimately. The primary type is DiffTest in difftest.go following the naming conventions, and the runner acts as a test helper that uses testing.T for reporting and runs each case as a subtest. I'm using os/exec.CommandContext to invoke both binaries with a passed context for timeout control.\n\nNow looking at the acceptance criteria: the DiffTest struct needs all the fields from the architecture spec, RunDiffTests should accept the test harness, binary paths, and test cases while reporting results, failure messages must include the input arguments and both expected and actual outputs, and the package should compile cleanly.\n\nI should verify I'm not duplicating closed issues or reimplementing existing code—the project context shows no closed issues to worry about, and while the code exists on the generation-merged tags, it hasn't been started on the working branch yet, so I need to create pkg/testutils/ from scratch. Let me finalize the proposal by summarizing the project state, reasoning through priorities, and making sure the YAML is valid.\n\nI'm constrained to exactly 1 task and must only propose for release 00.0 use cases. Looking at the three non-excluded foundational libraries—testutils, sys, and format—testutils is the most critical since it's the verification harness that all other packages depend on, so that's what I should propose. is possible. sys and format are needed by specific utilities but testutils is needed by ALL utilities.\n\nActually, per P8, the canonical ordering is alphabetically by primary file path when other rules don't decide. The paths are:\n- pkg/format/ \n- pkg/sys/\n- pkg/testutils/\n\nSo format comes first alphabetically. But P5 says foundational code before features. testutils is the testing infrastructure - it's arguably the most foundational.\n\nHmm, but P8 says \"Libraries and shared packages before consumers.\" None of these consume each other, so they're equal under that rule. Then the tiebreaker is alphabetical: format first.\n\nBut wait - there's another consideration. The pipeline flow is: extract → specify → synthesize → verify. The verification stage uses pkg/testutils/. For the pipeline to work end-to-end, testutils needs to exist first. This makes testutils more foundational than format or sys.\n\nI think testutils is the right choice as it's the verification mechanism that everything depends on. Re-reading P5, it emphasizes \"foundational code before features,\" and among foundational packages, the testing harness is the most critical since it gates all other code. I'm going with testutils.\n\nNow for the file count - the constraint asks for 5-7 files, but I'm looking at just 2 files (difftest.go and runner.go) for a 150-250 line implementation, which seems reasonable for this scope.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20542,"cache_read_input_tokens":14011,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20542},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06262b38-f8ea-4448-bd0e-ce919c2e2e61","uuid":"1b6c91c1-2497-4813-93cc-e55bd87b4f89"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RgPhZ4nadRZ9DY3ha1ZnsN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that derives formal specs from C source code (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils), generates Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies parity through differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go packages), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstraction), `pkg/format/` (shared formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, excluded from generation), and `docs/` (specs, excluded from generation).\n\n**Current state:** All specifications are written. Generated code (4,586 LOC) exists only at generation-merged tags from previous pipeline runs — the working branch has no Go code. Every use case in the code_status shows `codestatus: not started`.\n\n**Current release:** Release 00.0 (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure) with four use cases:\n- `rel00.0-uc001-magefiles` — **excluded** by DD7 (magefiles/ not a generation target)\n- `rel00.0-uc002-format` — pkg/format/, code not started\n- `rel00.0-uc003-sys` — pkg/sys/, code not started\n- `rel00.0-uc004-testutils` — pkg/testutils/, code not started\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThree non-excluded use cases remain in rel00.0: format, sys, and testutils. None depend on each other directly. However, `pkg/testutils/` is the differential testing harness — it is the primary verification mechanism (DD2) that every `cmd/` package needs to pass acceptance. Without it, no subsequent utility can be verified. This makes it the most foundational package in the pipeline.\n\nPer P5 (foundational code before features) and the project's own lifecycle (verification gates all code), `pkg/testutils/` should be built first.\n\nPer P2 (split implementation and tests), this task covers types + implementation only (combined since the DiffTest struct is small). Tests would follow in a subsequent batch.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"pkg/testutils DiffTest struct and runner -- rel00.0-uc004-testutils\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component, DiffTest data structure definition, DD2 differential testing, DD6 locale handling)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find the testutils PRD -- filename matches prd*testutils*.yaml)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc004-testutils.yaml (use case for this task)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-00.0.yaml (test suite covering rel00.0 use cases)\n      - go.mod (module path for import references)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: DiffTest struct, DiffResult type, RunDiffTests public API, normalization hook type\n      - path: pkg/testutils/runner.go\n        action: create\n        note: Internal binary execution, output capture, comparison, and divergence reporting\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Define the DiffTest struct in pkg/testutils/ with fields for input\n          arguments ([]string), stdin (string or io.Reader), environment variables\n          ([]string), expected exit code (int), and a test name (string), per the\n          ARCHITECTURE.yaml data structures section which specifies \"DiffTest: Go\n          struct in pkg/testutils/ defining a single differential test case with\n          fields for input arguments, stdin, environment, expected exit code, and\n          expected output.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement a runner function that executes both the Go binary under test\n          and the reference GNU binary with identical stdin, arguments, and\n          environment. Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each execution.\n          Use os/exec to invoke both binaries.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Compare stdout, stderr, and exit code between the Go binary and the\n          reference binary. When any of the three diverge, report a test failure\n          that includes the input arguments that triggered the divergence, the\n          expected output (from the reference binary), and the actual output\n          (from the Go binary).\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Support normalization hooks -- caller-provided functions with signature\n          func(string) string -- applied to stdout and stderr before comparison,\n          for known acceptable differences such as timestamps or binary name in\n          error messages.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Primary type is DiffTest; primary source file is difftest.go per\n          ARCHITECTURE.yaml file naming convention (testutils/testutils.go is\n          explicitly forbidden; file is named after the primary exported type).\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          The runner is a test helper called from _test.go files in cmd/ packages.\n          It accepts a *testing.T for failure reporting. Each DiffTest case runs\n          as a t.Run subtest named after the DiffTest.Name field so failures are\n          individually identifiable.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          Set LC_ALL=C in the environment for every binary execution per\n          ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD6 (all differential tests run with LC_ALL=C so Go\n          and reference binary outputs agree). Merge LC_ALL=C with any\n          caller-provided environment variables, with LC_ALL=C taking precedence.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: |\n          DiffTest struct is exported from pkg/testutils with all fields described\n          in ARCHITECTURE.yaml (input arguments, stdin, environment, expected exit\n          code, test name).\n      - id: AC2\n        text: |\n          RunDiffTests is an exported function that accepts *testing.T, the Go\n          binary path, the reference binary path, an optional slice of\n          normalization functions, and a slice of DiffTest cases.\n      - id: AC3\n        text: |\n          When stdout, stderr, or exit code diverge between the two binaries, the\n          test failure message includes the input arguments, the expected value\n          (reference binary), and the actual value (Go binary).\n      - id: AC4\n        text: |\n          Package compiles cleanly -- go build ./pkg/testutils/... and\n          go vet ./pkg/testutils/... produce no errors.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20542,"cache_read_input_tokens":14011,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20542},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06262b38-f8ea-4448-bd0e-ce919c2e2e61","uuid":"030cafe6-cab6-46b6-801a-159e977df87f"}
